This file describes how the ArtiSynth online manuals are set up on the
ArtiSynth webserver, and what documentation maintainers need to do
when documentation changes or manuals are added or removed. It
replaces INFOCENTER_README: the Eclipse InfoCenter that used to serve
the manuals was retired on 2026-11-12.
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Overview
--------

The online manuals at

https://www.artisynth.org/manuals/

are a static website, referred to here as DocNav. It shows the
LaTeXML-generated HTML for each manual inside a page with a banner, a
collapsible table of contents, and a full-text search box. There is no
server process to run, start, or restart: Apache serves everything as
plain files.

The table of contents and the search index are generated by the
script bin/makeDocNav, which must be run whenever the documentation
HTML changes. That is the main thing maintainers need to remember.
Which manuals appear, and in what order, is set by the plain text
file doc/docnav/contents.txt.

The ArtiSynth webserver is currently research.hct.ece.ubc.ca, and
ArtiSynth web materials are located under /var/www/artisynth.org. All
paths below are relative to that directory unless they start with /.

Location of Files
-----------------

doc/html/

    The documentation HTML, one subdirectory per manual (modelguide,
    uiguide, maspack, ...). These pages are served unchanged at
    https://www.artisynth.org/doc/html/... Each manual directory
    contains a table of contents file "xxxToc.xml", plus an HTML
    contents page "xxxToc.html", both generated automatically when the
    documentation HTML is created.

bin/makeDocNav

    The script that generates the site (Python 3, no extra packages).
    Run "bin/makeDocNav --help" for its options.

manuals/

    The generated site: index.html, home.html, the UBC logo, the search
    index in pagefind/, and .htaccess. Everything in here is
    overwritten by makeDocNav; don't edit it by hand.

doc/docnav/

    DocNav's settings. Not readable from the web (its .htaccess denies
    access). Documentation uploads copy doc/html or a manual's folder
    under it, so they don't touch this directory; don't rsync all of
    doc/ with --delete.

    contents.txt       Which manuals appear, in what order, and the
                       banner title. This is the file maintainers
                       usually edit.
    installation.xml   How the installation guides are grouped in the
                       table of contents (see "Installation Guides").
    manuals.htaccess   Redirects for old InfoCenter URLs. makeDocNav
                       copies it to manuals/.htaccess.
    UBCLogoTrans.gif   The logo shown in the banner.

packages/pagefind-1.5.2/pagefind

    The Pagefind program used to build the search index.

doc/.htaccess

    Rewrite rules for the short URLs https://www.artisynth.org/doc/info/xxx
    (see "Short URLs").

Updating Documentation
----------------------

Documentation HTML is copied to the server with rsync, either all of
doc/html or a single manual's directory, followed by an ssh command
that runs bin/fixMagicPerms to fix ownership and permissions. Add
bin/makeDocNav to the end of that ssh command, for example:

  ssh <server> 'cd /var/www/artisynth.org && bin/fixMagicPerms doc/html/modelguide && bin/makeDocNav'

makeDocNav always rebuilds the whole table of contents and search
index, whichever manual changed. It takes a few seconds. It should be
run after every update, including updates that only change page
content, since the search index is built from the page content.

makeDocNav prints a summary such as

  makeDocNav: 8 books, 996 topics -> /var/www/artisynth.org/manuals
  makeDocNav: indexed 237 pages -> /var/www/artisynth.org/manuals/pagefind

If it cannot read a table of contents file or index a page, it prints
a warning and exits with a non-zero status, so the ssh command will
report a failure. New files are written under temporary names and
then renamed, and if the search index can't be built the previous
index is left in place, so readers never see a half-built site.

makeDocNav makes its output group-writable, so any member of the
group www-data can run it again later.

Testing Changes
---------------

To try out a change to the contents (for example a new manual or a
reordering) without changing the live site, generate a copy into
another directory:

  bin/makeDocNav --out manuals-test

and view it at https://www.artisynth.org/manuals-test/. When it looks
right, run bin/makeDocNav without --out to update the live site.

Adding a New Document
---------------------

1) Create the document's HTML in its own directory under doc/html,
for example doc/html/newguide, including the generated
newguideToc.xml and newguideToc.html files, and copy it to the
server as usual.

2) Add a line for the document to doc/docnav/contents.txt, at the
position where it should appear in the table of contents. The line
is the path of its table of contents file, relative to doc/html:

    newguide/newguideToc.xml

The title shown in the contents comes from the label attribute of the
<toc> element in newguideToc.xml, i.e. the document's title.

3) Run bin/makeDocNav. The document then appears in the contents, in
the search index, and in the "All manuals" search filter.

4) Optionally, add a short URL for it to doc/.htaccess (see "Short
URLs"), and a link on the wiki's Software/Documentation page.

Unlike the InfoCenter, there is no plugin.xml or helpData.xml entry
to add, no numbered nav/X position to work out, and nothing to
restart.

Removing a Document
-------------------

1) Remove its line from doc/docnav/contents.txt (or comment it out
with #).

2) Run bin/makeDocNav. The document disappears from the contents and
the search index.

3) If it has a short URL in doc/.htaccess, don't delete the rule:
change its target to https://www.artisynth.org/manuals/ (or to a
replacement document), since links to it may exist in the
documentation bundled with older releases.

4) Its HTML directory can then be deleted, or left in place if
direct links to it should keep working.

Reordering or Renaming Documents
--------------------------------

The order of the contents is the order of the lines in
doc/docnav/contents.txt. Rearrange them and run bin/makeDocNav.

A document's title in the contents is its title in the LaTeX source
(the label attribute in its xxxToc.xml). Change it there, regenerate
and upload the HTML, and run bin/makeDocNav.

Installation Guides
-------------------

The "Installation" entry at the top of the contents is defined by
doc/docnav/installation.xml, listed in contents.txt as
"installation.xml" (a contents.txt line naming a file in doc/docnav
is read from there rather than from doc/html). It groups the guides
as:

  Installation
    GitHub/Eclipse Installation:  Windows, MacOS, Linux
        (the single-page xxxGitEclipseInstall.html guides)
    General Installation:         Windows, MacOS, Linux
        (each guide's contents page, with its sections below it)

Each entry is a <topic> with either an href to a page, or a
<link toc="..."/> that pulls in another document's xxxToc.xml (both
paths relative to doc/). Edit this file to add or rearrange
installation guides, then run bin/makeDocNav.

Changing the Banner
-------------------

The banner title is set by the "title:" line in
doc/docnav/contents.txt, currently

    title: ArtiSynth Manuals and Guides - current branch (3.9+)

Edit it (for example when the version changes) and run
bin/makeDocNav. The logo is doc/docnav/UBCLogoTrans.gif. The page
layout and styling are defined in bin/makeDocNav itself.

Linking to the Manuals
----------------------

https://www.artisynth.org/manuals/

    The start page.

https://www.artisynth.org/manuals/?topic=html/uiguide/S3.html#SS1

    Opens a specific page (path relative to doc/) inside the
    contents/search frame. The address bar shows a link of this form
    for whatever page is being viewed, so it can be copied from there.

https://www.artisynth.org/doc/html/uiguide/S3.html

    The page on its own, without the frame.

Short URLs
----------

doc/.htaccess maps short URLs of the form

https://www.artisynth.org/doc/info/<document>

to each document's contents page in DocNav, for example

RewriteRule ^info/uiguide(/(uiguide\.html)?)?$ https://www.artisynth.org/manuals/?topic=html/uiguide/uiguideToc.html [R,L]

These short URLs are used by links inside the manuals themselves, by
the wiki, and by the documentation bundled with ArtiSynth releases
since 3.4, so the existing rules must be kept, even for documents
that are removed (point them at /manuals/ instead). To add one for a
new document, copy an existing rule and change the names.

Old InfoCenter URLs
-------------------

Links to the old InfoCenter (https://www.artisynth.org/manuals/index.jsp?topic=...,
.../manuals/topic/..., .../manuals/nav/N, and anything under
https://research.hct.ece.ubc.ca/artisynth/doc/) are redirected to the
equivalent DocNav pages. The rules are in doc/docnav/manuals.htaccess
(copied to manuals/.htaccess by makeDocNav) and, for research.hct, in
/etc/apache2/sites-available/003-research.hct.conf.

The nav/N rules refer to positions in the InfoCenter's contents as it
was when it was retired. They are fixed and should not be changed
when documents are added, removed, or reordered.

Search
------

Search is provided by Pagefind (https://pagefind.app), which builds a
static index that runs in the reader's browser. makeDocNav indexes
only the pages listed in the contents, not the single-page versions
of each manual (e.g. uiguide.html, which would duplicate results) or
the Java API docs. It indexes copies of the pages, marked up to give
the manual name and section titles; the files in doc/html are not
changed.

Searches match pages containing the words typed, including word
prefixes ("rend" finds "render") and other forms of a word
("rendering" finds "render"). Quotes match an exact phrase. Case,
punctuation, and accents are ignored. Matches in headings rank
highest. The manual drop-down limits a search to one document.

To upgrade Pagefind, download the new release's
pagefind-<version>-x86_64-unknown-linux-musl.tar.gz from
https://github.com/Pagefind/pagefind/releases, check it against the
.sha256 file published with it, unpack the pagefind binary into
packages/pagefind-<version>/, change the PAGEFIND setting near the
top of bin/makeDocNav, and run bin/makeDocNav.

Connection to the Webserver
---------------------------

Nothing in the old InfoCenter installation
(packages/eclipse-platform-4.4.2) is used by DocNav.

No special Apache configuration is needed: manuals/ is an ordinary
directory under the www.artisynth.org document root, and
manuals/.htaccess sets index.html as its index page (the site default
is pmwiki.php). On research.hct.ece.ubc.ca, /artisynth/doc/... is
redirected to www.artisynth.org by rewrite rules in
003-research.hct.conf.

The InfoCenter's systemd service, eclipse-infocenter, is disabled
and should not be re-enabled.
